      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the certificate.\n\nThis is part 4 of 4 — pages 11-13 of an NMi OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9 (water meters, R49).\n\nContent:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 9\n- Project number: 2563125\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Page total: 13\n- Pages 11, 12, 13\n\nTables 9-12: General characteristics of the indicating device, by meter size (DN), bore type (reduced/full), power type (AC mains vs battery/renewable).\n\nTable 9: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: max range 9999999 m³, min range 99999 m³, VSI max 0.0001 m³\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300-DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 10: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 11: Full bore – AC mains powered\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- (page 12 continues) DN350-DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700-DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350-DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nNote: Table 11's first rows are on page 11, and page 12 has an unlabeled table continuing it (DN350, DN400...). So Table 11 spans pages 11-12.\n\nTable 12: Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200-DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900-DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nProduction locations:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n- ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\n- ABB India Limited, Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\n\nCertificate history / revision history:\n- Initial, 14 August 2020, First issue.\n- 1, 30 September 2020, Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\n- 2, 24 December 2020, Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\n- 3, 02 August 2021, Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\n- 4, 26 August 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\n- 5, 23 November 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\n- 6, 20 May 2022, Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\n- 7, 30 August 2022, Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\n- 8, 3 July 2023, Software update\n- 9, 22 September 2023, Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device. Software update\n\nNow, the schema:\n\ncertificate: number, scheme (A), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id.\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- revision 9 — the schema has revision_history but for the certificate itself... The number says \"revision 9\". Hmm, the certificate schema doesn't have a revision field. I could include revision_history from the table. The revision number could be noted. There's no field for current revision in the certificate schema; but revision_history captures it. Date issued: 22 September 2023 (date of revision 9) — but that's inferred, not directly stated. Actually the fragment only shows \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9\". The date of revision 9 is 22 September 2023 per the history table. I could set date_issued to \"2023-09-22\" as it corresponds to the current revision... but rule 8 says don't fabricate. The date isn't explicitly stated as issuance date. However it's strongly implied. I'll include it since the latest revision date is the issue date; it's a reasonable inference. Hmm, being safe: I could omit it. But I think including date_issued: \"2023-09-22\" is defensible. Actually, let me be careful — the instruction says \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The date 22 September 2023 is present in the markdown as the date of revision 9. The current revision is 9. So date_issued = 2023-09-22 is a direct mapping. I'll include it.\n- scheme: \"A\" (from the number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, the \"-A-\" indicates scheme A). I'll include scheme: \"A\".\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- project_number: \"2563125\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present in fragment. NMi is the issuer but no ID. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" — address not present. Include name only, oiml_issuer_id omitted.\n\napplicants: not present in this fragment. Omit.\n\nmanufacturers: not present in this fragment (production locations are different — those are production sites). Hmm. Should production locations be manufacturers? The schema doesn't have a production_locations field. Options: omit, or include in manufacturers[]. Since the schema doesn't cover production locations, and rule 8 says don't fabricate fields... But these are ABB entities producing the water meter. The certified type's manufacturer is presumably ABB (from earlier parts). I think I could capture production locations as manufacturers[] entries since they are the entities manufacturing. But strictly they're \"production locations\". Alternatively, I could add them under manufacturers with a note. The schema is layered and prescriptive; adding a non-schema key like \"production_locations\" is risky but the instructions say conform to schema for content present. I think best: include them as manufacturers[] since they are manufacturing entities. Actually wait — part 4 fragment only; applicants/manufacturers from earlier parts would be in earlier fragments. If I include production locations as manufacturers, and the earlier fragment also has manufacturers, there'd be duplication. But the caller presumably merges. Hmm.\n\nThe instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nProduction locations are present. The closest schema slot is manufacturers[]. I'll include them there. Alternatively omit to be safe. I think including them is more useful; they are legal manufacturing entities. I'll include them under manufacturers[].\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters). type_designations: the fragment mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" in revision history — \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\". That's a type designation mentioned. Include? It's in the revision history. The certified type is the ABB AquaMaster water meter presumably. I could include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm, it's the mobile comms variant added in rev 9. I'll include it as it's a type identifier mentioned in the fragment.\n\nmodule_designation: not applicable here — omit or null. Rule: omit if not present.\n\nmodel_family: the meter sizes DN40...DN1800 are model variants. This fragment is about indicating device characteristics per meter size. Should I populate model_family.models with all DN sizes? The models here are meter sizes: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\nThat's 29 sizes. But the fragment's tables are about indicating device characteristics grouped by size ranges. The tables themselves are config-level: axes = bore (reduced/full) and power (AC mains / battery-renewable).\n\nHow to structure? Options:\n\n1. matrix_tables: preserve the 4 tables as-is with name, columns, rows.\n2. config_level characteristics: attribute per table, axis = \"meter_size\" with conditions.\n\nGiven the tables group DN sizes into rows with identical values, the natural representation is matrix_tables with rows keyed by DN groups, plus maybe config_level entries.\n\nThe config axes: \"bore\" (reduced bore / full bore) and \"power_supply\" (AC mains powered / battery-renewable energy powered). Each table = one combination.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: indicating_range_maximum, axis: \"bore_and_power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"reduced_bore, ac_mains\", value: 9999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, ...]. But the value varies by meter size too — three dimensions. Hmm.\n\nActually within each table, max indicating range is 9999999 for most, but full bore tables have 99999999 and 999999999 for larger sizes. So value depends on size group.\n\nBest approach: matrix_tables[] with 4 entries (tables 9-12), each with rows where each row is a DN group with values. Plus config_level characteristics for the three attributes with conditions combining bore/power/size? That gets messy.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest per schema:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"General characteristics of the indicating device — Reduced bore, AC mains powered (Table 9)\",\n    \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum\", \"indicating_range_minimum\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"],\n    \"column_units\": [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n    \"rows\": [\n      {\"meter_size\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.0001},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  ...\n]\n\nBut rule 1 says no LaTeX, and \"m³\" — is ³ ok? It's Unicode superscript, not LaTeX. The R137 example uses \"m³/h\" so yes.\n\nAlso the page-12 unlabeled table is a continuation of Table 11 (Full bore – AC mains powered). I should merge it into the Table 11 matrix_table. Yes — the table on page 12 with DN350-DN1800 rows has the same columns as Table 11, and it appears before Table 12 header. So merge.\n\nSo Table 11 full rows:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 12 rows:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nNow, should I also populate model_level or config_level characteristics? The schema example for R137 populated model_level from matrix tables. Here the variation is by DN size AND power/bore config. I could do config_level with entries like:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\", \"axis\": \"bore_and_power_supply\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"reduced_bore, ac_mains, DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}, ...]}\n\nThat's very verbose. Alternatively, one config_level entry per attribute per table. That'd be 12 config entries with grouped conditions.\n\nHonestly, the matrix_tables preserve the data faithfully. I could add config_level for a compact summary, but the risk of error/verbosity is high. The rules say config_level for attributes varying by configuration. Here attributes vary by configuration AND size. The matrix_tables are the right home. I think I'll do matrix_tables with 4 entries and skip config_level/model_level for these, since a faithful extraction is via matrix_tables. Actually, hmm — should I also include model_family with the DN sizes? The fragment alone defines models as meter sizes DN40–DN1800. Including model_family.models with 29 entries would be consistent with the R137 pattern. But the fragment doesn't state these are the full certified models... it's implied (full-bore up to DN1800, reduced bore up to DN600 per revision history).\n\nI think including model_family with the meter sizes as model_id is reasonable and follows the guidance (\"populate model_family.models\" when matrix tables by size exist). Let me enumerate the sizes: from tables:\nDN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 (reduced bore up to DN600), DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\n29 models. model_id: \"DN40\", etc.\n\nShould I include attributes per model? That would be redundant with matrix_tables. I'll just list models.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to include model_family at all. The schema says \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". It does — 29 DN sizes. I'll include with models list. But there's risk the caller merges with earlier fragment's model_family which might have family_name \"AquaMaster 4\". I'll set family_name: null since not in fragment. Hmm, revision history mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\". That's a specific variant designation. The family might be \"AquaMaster4\". It's mentioned in the fragment (\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant\"). Could set family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"? That's an inference. The mention is \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\" — the model designation is \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\". I'd rather not guess family_name; leave null and put \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" in type_designations.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition... The number is R49/2013 — edition 2013. The schema wants edition (int). R49/2013 suggests edition 2013. Hmm, is that fabrication? The certificate number itself encodes it. I think edition: 2013 is reasonable. But careful — earlier parts would have this. Since the number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" is in this fragment, I can derive recommendation id R49 and edition 2013. accuracy_classes: revision history mentions accuracy class 1 (\"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\") and E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. R49 water meter accuracy classes... The fragment mentions \"accuracy class 1\" and \"E2 class and O class\". Hmm, E2/O are temperature classes for R49 actually — in OIML R49, water meters have accuracy classes 1 and 2, and temperature classes like O, T50 etc. Actually \"E2\" might be electromagnetic class... In R49-2: metrological classes... Let me not overthink. Should I include accuracy_classes? The fragment mentions class 1 (accuracy) in revision 6. E2 and O are classes for the flow transmitter (likely electromagnetic/temperature classes). I'll include accuracy_classes: [1] — hmm, but the cert might also cover class 2 from earlier parts. The fragment only shows class 1 addition. Risky. Maybe omit accuracy_classes entirely since the fragment doesn't clearly list the full set. Actually revision 6 says \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\" — meaning class 1 was added for those sizes; class 2 presumably already there from initial. Including only [1] would be misleading. I'll omit accuracy_classes.\n\nShould I include recommendation at all? id \"R49\" is derivable from the number. I'll include {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\"}. Scheme A from certificate number. Edition 2013 from \"R49/2013\". I think that's safe enough. Hmm, the scheme in the number — \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the \"-A-\" is indeed scheme A for OIML-CS. Yes.\n\ntest_reports: not present. Omit.\n\nrevision_history: extract all 10 entries. revision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\"...\"9\". Dates: convert to ISO? The schema shows \"date\" — I'll use ISO format \"2020-08-14\" etc. Or keep as text? Example earlier schema: revision (string), date, changes. I'll use ISO dates: 2020-08-14, 2020-09-30, 2020-12-24, 2021-08-02, 2021-08-26, 2021-11-23, 2022-05-20, 2022-08-30, 2023-07-03, 2023-09-22.\n\nChanges text: keep as given, clean en-dashes: \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n\nfootnotes: none present. Omit.\n\ncharacteristics type_level: nothing whole-cert in this fragment. Omit.\n\nNow the matrix_tables details. Column names: canonical snake_case: \"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum_value\" or \"indicating_range_maximum\"? Headers: \"Indicating range (maximum value) [m³]\" → indicating_range_maximum; \"Indicating range (minimum value) [m³]\" → indicating_range_minimum; \"Verification scale interval (maximum value) [m³]\" → verification_scale_interval_maximum.\n\nUnits: m³ for all three.\n\nValues: numbers with decimal conversion: 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,001 → 0.001; 0,01 → 0.01; 0,1 → 0.1; 1 → 1.\n\nRows: meter_size as string like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\". Alternatively expand each DN into separate rows? The R137 example kept one row per model. Here grouping is how the doc presents. To be faithful and compact, keep grouped strings. But then model_level linkage is harder. I'll keep grouped rows in matrix_tables and also expand into config_level? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: should the meter_size column be expanded to individual DN entries to align with model_family models? That would make ~90 rows total across 4 tables. That's a lot but more machine-usable. The R137 example expanded rows per model because each row was one size. Here rows are groups. Expanding duplicates info faithfully. I think keeping the grouped form is more faithful and compact; consumers can split on \", \". I'll keep groups as strings.\n\nHmm, but the schema says rows: \"object per row\". Grouped strings are fine.\n\nAlso include a \"description\" for each matrix_table noting the config: e.g. \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered indicating device characteristics by meter size\".\n\nTable names: \"Table 9 — General characteristics of the indicating device (Reduced bore – AC mains powered)\". Keep simple.\n\nShould the page-12 continuation be its own table or merged? Merged into Table 11 entry, with description noting it spans pages 11-12. I'll merge and note.\n\nNow characteristics config_level: I think adding compact config_level entries would be valuable given the schema emphasizes matrices. But the variation here is truly 3-dimensional (bore × power × size). The cleanest config_level representation: attribute per combination, axis \"bore_and_power_supply\", conditions \"reduced_bore_ac_mains, DN40-DN65\". That's essentially re-encoding the tables. I'll skip config_level and rely on matrix_tables — the rules say matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables \"that don't fit elsewhere\". This fits that clause: 3 dimensions doesn't fit config_level (single axis).\n\nActually, wait — could I treat the axis as \"meter_size\" and emit config_level per table? E.g.:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\", \"axis\": \"meter_size (reduced bore, AC mains)\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}, ...]}\n\nThat's 12 entries × ~5 values = 60 value objects. Versus matrix_tables 4 tables × ~6 rows. The matrix_tables approach is cleaner and explicitly allowed. Go with matrix_tables only.\n\nHmm, but the instruction #4 says \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". For R137. Here the matrix columns are config-specific indicating device characteristics, not model attributes per se. But I could populate model_level for attributes that... no, values vary within same size across configs (e.g., VSI for DN200: reduced/AC = 0.001, reduced/battery = 0.01, full/AC = 0.01, full/battery = 0.01). So it's config-dependent, not pure model-level. matrix_tables alone is right. I'll still populate model_family.models.\n\nNow manufacturers vs production locations. Let me decide: include a manufacturers[] array with the four ABB entities? The heading is \"Production location\" — \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". These are manufacturing entities. The schema has no production_locations field. I'll include them under manufacturers[] — it's the best-fitting slot. Some might argue omit. I'll include, since extraction value > strictness here. Actually... rule 8 \"Don't fabricate\" — putting production locations in \"manufacturers\" is a mapping judgment, not fabrication. I'll do it.\n\ncertified_type: category — R49 water meter. \"Water meter\". type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm, the revision says \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\". So type designation AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z. I'll include it. description: omit (none in fragment beyond tables).\n\nIssuing authority name: \"NMi\" — appears in headers. Include name: \"NMi\". Other fields absent.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2023-09-22\" (date of revision 9). I'll include.\n\nAlso certificate.page_total: 13 (\"Page 11 of 13\", \"Page 12 of 13\", \"Page 13 of 13\").\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". scheme: \"A\". project_number: \"2563125\". member_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\nLet me now write the JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2563125\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-09-22\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  